## 7. Attar education

**What an attar is.** An attar (also *ittar*) is a concentrated fragrance oil distilled from botanicals — flowers, woods, resins, spices, herbs — traditionally into a sandalwood oil base. It contains no alcohol. It is applied in drops rather than sprayed.

**Attar versus perfume.** A conventional eau de parfum is typically 15-20 percent fragrance concentrate dissolved in ethanol. The alcohol flashes off on contact with skin, projecting the opening notes sharply and then fading. An attar is essentially undiluted aromatic material in an oil carrier. It opens quietly, warms with the body over time, sits closer to the skin, and generally persists far longer. See [Attar vs Perfume]({{ base }}/blogs/news/blogs-fragrance-guide-attar-vs-perfume-difference).

**Why alcohol-free matters in India.** High ambient heat accelerates alcohol evaporation, which shortens the life of a sprayed perfume and can amplify its harshness. Alcohol is also drying and can irritate sensitive or eczema-prone skin. Oil-based attars avoid both problems and are acceptable in religious and ritual contexts where alcohol is not. See [Alcohol-Free Perfumes in India]({{ base }}/blogs/news/blogs-news-alcohol-free-perfumes-in-india-why-attar-is-better) and [Alcohol-Free Perfume for Sensitive Skin]({{ base }}/blogs/news/alcohol-free-perfume-for-sensitive-skin).

**How to apply attar.** Apply a small amount to pulse points — wrists, sides of the neck, behind the ears — on clean, moisturised skin. Warmth drives diffusion, so pulse points matter. Do not rub the wrists together: friction and heat fracture the top notes and shorten the development. Let the oil settle for a few minutes before judging the scent. See [How to Apply Attar Correctly]({{ base }}/blogs/news/how-to-apply-attar-correctly).

**Longevity.** Oil evaporates far more slowly than ethanol, so attar remains detectable on skin and clothing for many hours. Moisturised skin holds fragrance longer than dry skin. See [Why Pure Attar Lasts Longer Than Perfume]({{ base }}/blogs/news/why-pure-attar-lasts-longer-than-perfume).

**Layering.** Attars can be combined to build a personal accord — typically one dominant floral or woody base plus a smaller accent of resin, spice or musk. See [Attar Layering Guide]({{ base }}/blogs/news/attar-layering-guide-create-your-own-signature-fragrance-naturally).

**Storage.** Keep bottles closed, upright, away from direct sunlight and heat. Good attars mature rather than spoil.

## 8. Ayurveda and ritual education

In Ayurvedic thought, aroma acts on the mind and the doshas directly, which is why specific botanicals are prescribed for specific states: sandalwood and vetiver to cool and settle pitta, warm resins and spices to steady vata, bright citrus and camphoraceous notes to lift kapha. Fragrance is treated as a therapeutic input rather than an accessory.

Ritual use in the Indian tradition includes anointing before prayer or meditation, marking festivals and rites of passage, and daily practice tied to the day's planetary ruler. Incense serves a parallel function, purifying and framing a space before practice.

