## 13. Ingredient glossary

| Ingredient | Also known as | Profile and traditional use |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Oud | Agarwood, oudh | Resinous heartwood of infected *Aquilaria* trees. Deep, smoky, animalic, extremely long-lasting. The most prized material in Middle Eastern and Indian perfumery. |
| Sandalwood | Chandan | Creamy, soft, milky wood. The classical base oil for deg-bhapka attars and a cooling agent in Ayurveda. |
| Mogra | Indian jasmine, *Jasminum sambac* | Intensely sweet white floral, narcotic and rich. Central to Indian garlands and temple offerings. |
| Rose | Gulab, *Rosa damascena* | Fresh, honeyed, slightly green floral. The benchmark of classical attar-making. |
| Kesar | Saffron | Leathery, hay-like, warm spice. Rare, costly and associated with royalty and auspiciousness. |
| Loban | Frankincense, benzoin | Balsamic resin with smoky sweetness. Burned in temples; used to mark sacred space. |
| Khus | Vetiver | Cool, earthy, rooty and green. Traditionally used in Indian summer for its cooling effect. |
| Musk | Kasturi (modern versions are plant or synthetic derived) | Soft, skin-like, warm. Extends longevity and rounds sharp edges. |
| Amber | Amber accord | Warm, resinous, powdery-sweet. An accord rather than a single raw material. |
| Champa | Nag champa, frangipani-magnolia accord | Creamy floral with a powdery finish. Iconic in Indian incense. |
| Vanilla | — | Sweet, balsamic, gourmand. Softens woods and resins. |
| Tobacco | — | Dry, honeyed, leafy warmth. Pairs with vanilla and woods. |
| Lotus | Kamal | Clean, watery, serene floral. Strong sacred symbolism in Indian tradition. |
| Lavender | — | Herbaceous, calming, camphoraceous. Used for sleep and stress relief. |

